::ALG1_U03_L05_Q01::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.2] Write the equation of a line with slope 2 and y-intercept (0, 7). {
=A. y = 2x + 7 #Correct. Use y = mx + b with m = 2 and b = 7.
~B. y = 7x + 2 #This reverses the slope and y-intercept.
~C. y = 2x - 7 #The y-intercept should be positive 7.
~D. y = x + 9 #This adds the given values instead of placing them in slope-intercept form.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_Q02::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.2] A line has slope -3 and passes through (0, 5). Which equation matches? {
~A. y = 5x - 3 #This reverses the slope and intercept.
~B. y = 3x + 5 #The slope sign is wrong.
=C. y = -3x + 5 #Correct. The point (0, 5) gives b = 5.
~D. y = -5x + 3 #This uses the wrong slope and intercept.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_Q03::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.2] A line has slope 4 and passes through (1, 9). What is its equation? {
~A. y = 4x + 9 #This treats the y-value as the y-intercept.
=B. y = 4x + 5 #Correct. 9 = 4(1) + b, so b = 5.
~C. y = x + 8 #This uses slope 1 instead of 4.
~D. y = 4x - 5 #The intercept sign is wrong.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_Q04::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.5] Which equation is parallel to y = 6x - 1 and passes through (0, 3)? {
~A. y = -6x + 3 #Parallel lines need the same slope, not the opposite slope.
~B. y = 3x + 6 #This reverses the slope and y-intercept.
~C. y = 6x - 1 #This is the original line, not the new one through (0, 3).
=D. y = 6x + 3 #Correct. Same slope 6 and y-intercept 3.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_Q05::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.5] What is the slope of a line perpendicular to a line with slope 5? {
=A. -1/5 #Correct. Perpendicular slopes are negative reciprocals.
~B. 5 #This slope would make a parallel line.
~C. 1/5 #This is the reciprocal but the sign must change.
~D. -5 #This changes the sign but not the reciprocal.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_Q06::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.2] A gym charges $30 to join and $12 each month. Which equation models total cost y after x months? {
~A. y = 30x + 12 #This reverses the fixed cost and monthly rate.
=B. y = 12x + 30 #Correct. The monthly rate is 12 and the starting cost is 30.
~C. y = 42x #This treats the fixed cost and rate as one rate.
~D. y = x + 42 #This uses a rate of 1 month instead of 12.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_Q07::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.2] A line has slope -2 and passes through (3, -1). What is its equation? {
~A. y = -2x - 1 #This treats the point's y-value as the y-intercept.
~B. y = 2x - 7 #The slope sign is wrong.
~C. y = -2x - 7 #This subtracts where the intercept should be 5.
=D. y = -2x + 5 #Correct. -1 = -2(3) + b, so b = 5.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_Q08::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.5] Which equation is perpendicular to y = -4x + 2 and has y-intercept (0, -3)? {
~A. y = -4x - 3 #This is parallel to the given line.
~B. y = 4x - 3 #This changes the sign but does not use the reciprocal.
~C. y = -1/4x - 3 #This uses the reciprocal but the sign is wrong.
=D. y = 1/4x - 3 #Correct. The negative reciprocal of -4 is 1/4.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_Q09::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.2] Write the equation of the line with slope 1/2 and y-intercept (0, -6). {
=A. y = 1/2x - 6 #Correct. The slope is 1/2 and b = -6.
~B. y = -6x + 1/2 #This reverses slope and intercept.
~C. y = 1/2x + 6 #The intercept sign is wrong.
~D. y = 2x - 6 #This uses the reciprocal of the slope.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_Q10::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.2] A line with slope 7 passes through (2, 15). What is b in y = 7x + b? {
~A. 15 #This treats the y-coordinate as the intercept.
~B. 17 #This adds 2 to 15 instead of using the slope.
~C. 14 #This calculates 7(2) but does not solve for b.
=D. 1 #Correct. 15 = 14 + b, so b = 1.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_Q11::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.5] Which line is parallel to y = -x + 8? {
~A. y = x + 8 #This slope is positive 1, not -1.
=B. y = -x - 4 #Correct. Both lines have slope -1.
~C. y = 8x - 1 #This uses the intercept as the slope.
~D. y = 1/8x - 4 #This uses an unrelated reciprocal slope.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_Q12::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.2] A repair bill has a $40 service fee plus $25 per hour. Which equation models total cost y after x hours? {
~A. y = 40x + 25 #This reverses the service fee and hourly rate.
~B. y = 65x #This treats the total of the two numbers as an hourly rate.
=C. y = 25x + 40 #Correct. The rate is 25 per hour and the starting fee is 40.
~D. y = x + 65 #This uses a rate of 1 instead of 25.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_Q13::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.2] A line has slope 0 and passes through (0, -4). What is its equation? {
=A. y = -4 #Correct. A slope of 0 creates a horizontal line with constant y-value -4.
~B. x = -4 #This is a vertical line, not a line with slope 0.
~C. y = -4x #This has slope -4 and y-intercept 0.
~D. y = x - 4 #This has slope 1.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_Q14::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.5] What slope is perpendicular to 2/3? {
~A. 2/3 #This is the same slope, so it would be parallel.
~B. 3/2 #This is the reciprocal but the sign must change.
=C. -3/2 #Correct. The negative reciprocal of 2/3 is -3/2.
~D. -2/3 #This changes the sign but not the reciprocal.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_Q15::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.2] A line passes through (0, 8) and (2, 14). Which equation matches the line? {
~A. y = 8x + 14 #This reverses the intercept and a point value.
~B. y = 6x + 8 #The slope from the two points is 3, not 6.
~C. y = 3x + 14 #This uses the second point's y-value as the y-intercept.
=D. y = 3x + 8 #Correct. The slope is (14 - 8)/(2 - 0) = 3 and b = 8.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_Q16::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.2] A line has slope -1 and passes through (5, 2). What is its equation? {
~A. y = -x + 2 #This uses the point's y-value as the intercept.
=B. y = -x + 7 #Correct. 2 = -5 + b, so b = 7.
~C. y = x - 3 #The slope sign is wrong.
~D. y = -5x + 2 #This uses the x-coordinate as the slope.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_Q17::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.5] Which equation is parallel to y = 3/4x + 1 and has y-intercept (0, -2)? {
=A. y = 3/4x - 2 #Correct. Parallel lines keep slope 3/4 and use the new intercept.
~B. y = -3/4x - 2 #The slope sign is wrong for a parallel line.
~C. y = 4/3x - 2 #This uses the reciprocal slope.
~D. y = -4/3x - 2 #This is the perpendicular slope, not parallel.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_Q18::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.2] A car rental costs $18 plus $0.30 per mile. Which equation models total cost y after x miles? {
~A. y = 18x + 0.30 #This treats 18 as the per-mile rate.
=B. y = 0.30x + 18 #Correct. The cost grows by 0.30 per mile after the fixed fee.
~C. y = 18.30x #This treats the fixed fee and rate as one rate.
~D. y = x + 18.30 #This uses a per-mile rate of 1.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_Q19::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.2] A line has slope -5 and passes through (1, -9). What is the equation? {
~A. y = -5x - 9 #This treats the y-coordinate as the intercept.
~B. y = 5x - 14 #The slope sign is wrong.
=C. y = -5x - 4 #Correct. -9 = -5(1) + b, so b = -4.
~D. y = -4x - 5 #This reverses the slope and intercept.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_Q20::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.5] Which pair of slopes represents perpendicular lines? {
~A. 4 and 4 #Equal slopes make parallel lines.
~B. -4 and -4 #Equal slopes make parallel lines, even when negative.
~C. 4 and 1/4 #These are reciprocals but not negative reciprocals.
=D. 4 and -1/4 #Correct. Perpendicular slopes multiply to -1.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_Q21::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.2] A table starts at y = 9 when x = 0 and increases by 2 each time x increases by 1. Which equation matches? {
=A. y = 2x + 9 #Correct. The rate is 2 and the starting value is 9.
~B. y = 9x + 2 #This reverses the starting value and rate.
~C. y = 11x #This treats the sum as a rate.
~D. y = x + 11 #This uses a rate of 1.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_Q22::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.5] A line is perpendicular to y = 1/5x + 6 and passes through (0, 2). Which equation matches? {
~A. y = 1/5x + 2 #This is parallel to the given line.
~B. y = 5x + 2 #This uses the reciprocal but not the negative reciprocal.
=C. y = -5x + 2 #Correct. The negative reciprocal of 1/5 is -5.
~D. y = -1/5x + 2 #This changes the sign but not the reciprocal.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_Q23::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.2] A line passes through (4, 10) with slope 3. Which mistake should be avoided? {
~A. Substituting 4 for x #That is necessary because the point's x-coordinate is 4.
=B. Treating 10 as the y-intercept #Correct. The point (4, 10) is not on the y-axis, so b is not 10.
~C. Using y = mx + b #That is an appropriate form for writing a linear equation.
~D. Solving for b #Solving for b is the correct next step.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_Q24::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.2] Which equation has slope -2 and contains the point (6, 1)? {
~A. y = -2x + 1 #This treats the point's y-value as the intercept.
~B. y = 2x - 11 #The slope sign is wrong.
~C. y = -2x - 11 #This uses the wrong intercept sign.
=D. y = -2x + 13 #Correct. 1 = -2(6) + b, so b = 13.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_Q25::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.2] Which equation models a starting value of -3 and a rate of change of 8? {
=A. y = 8x - 3 #Correct. The rate is the slope and the starting value is the y-intercept.
~B. y = -3x + 8 #This reverses the rate and starting value.
~C. y = 8x + 3 #The starting value sign is wrong.
~D. y = x + 5 #This combines the numbers instead of using slope-intercept form.
}
